WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:08.170
VANESSA JULY: Hi, everyone.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.190
Thank you so much for
joining us this afternoon.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.350
My name is Vanessa July.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.210
And I'm a Cloud
Customer Engineer

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.580
for Higher Education at Google.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.240
That means that I work with
customers like yourselves

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.220
on applications that
touch the student

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.070
body, the staff
at an institution,

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:29.220
and even sometimes,
in use cases, distance

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.220
learning and online learning.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.980
So today, you'll hear
a little bit from me

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.550
on what Google's vision
for an AI-first institution

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.553
looks like.

00:00:40.553 --> 00:00:41.970
Most of the time,
actually, you'll

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.970
be hearing from people just
like yourselves and institutions

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.638
who have implemented
some of this technology

00:00:47.638 --> 00:00:49.680
and what that was like--
what that experience was

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.915
like for them and
some of these cases

00:00:52.915 --> 00:00:54.540
that came out of that
and, potentially,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.640
even some of the roadmap
opportunities that

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.440
exist for those institutions.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.850
So I think a lot of us here know
that the path for a student,

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.950
from prospective all the way
to employed professional,

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.350
is not one step.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.850
And so Google really
took this to heart.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.050
And we think of it and translate
it into a student lifecycle.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:20.850
And what that means
is, there are so

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.950
many places along
a student journey

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:25.510
that we can interject
with technology.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:27.930
And we think about how
we can use technology

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:31.650
to enrich that experience.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.330
This is much more than what
you see on the screen here.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.520
But we're going to focus on
steps two and a little bit more

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.840
on step four.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.240
When you have a student
that is at your university,

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.260
how do you help them?

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.380
And how do you deploy
an agent and not

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:48.870
just use ML as a buzzword?

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.020
How do you actually interject
that into the student lifecycle

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.460
to help that student as they
work through your university?

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.360
And so what you'll see
is Dialogflow is really

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.300
the agent that enables this.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:08.009
Very simply, Dialogflow is a
conversation-building tool.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.160
And it does so in
a very powerful way

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.890
by using natural
language processing

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.210
to take the way that a student
or a professor, a teacher,

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:21.690
or someone would speak
naturally, and translate that

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:23.400
into something
that you might have

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.380
heard before called intents.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.302
And you can think
of that by imagining

00:02:28.302 --> 00:02:29.760
all the different
ways that someone

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.690
might ask about the weather.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.160
So you can say, how
hot is it today?

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.960
Will it rain today?

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.230
What's the temperature
going to be like this week?

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.120
And traditionally,
it may have been

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.540
very rigid and
difficult for people

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.100
to interact with
an agent because it

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.670
required a very specific input.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:51.480
And it's also very
difficult for developers

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:55.530
to create that agent by
hard-coding very rigid language

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.020
and contexts into that agent.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.140
And so what Dialogflow
does is remove that,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.560
meaning that your user, your
student, your staff member,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.500
can interact the way that
we talk very naturally.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.600
And all of the natural
language processing

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.290
that is built into Dialogflow
translates that for you

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.030
into something
actionable in your bot.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.410
And so today,
you're going to hear

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.140
from three different
institutions on how they've

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.720
implemented a lot
of this technology

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.680
and how they were able to
build faster to iterate,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.530
to bring in student
input and focus

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:35.190
group input to deploy
something very quickly.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.640
You'll hear about how the
integrations that Dialogflow

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.610
presents to you, allows
you to meet your student,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.910
and to meet your staff
member where they are.

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:45.910
So this means they can
interact on their phones.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.085
They can interact in
the LMS, in the LRS.

00:03:48.085 --> 00:03:49.710
They can interact in
the student portal

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:52.350
how they're used to interacting.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.510
And you'll hear about
how, when you're

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.540
able to meet your students
and your staff where they are

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.070
and how they want to interact,
how that really helps you

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:05.283
to maximize those investments,
not just in technology,

00:04:05.283 --> 00:04:07.950
but also in the information that
you're able to collect and then

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:11.320
give back to your user base.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:14.870
And so with that, I'm going
to bring Greg Reynolds up

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:16.620
to the stage to tell
you about how they've

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.790
done that at Case Western.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.090
GREG REYNOLDS:
Thank you, Vanessa.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:26.260
Hello.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:27.760
My name is Gregory Reynolds.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.730
I'm an application developer
at Case Western Reserve

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:31.978
University.

00:04:31.978 --> 00:04:33.520
And for those of
you that don't know,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.360
Case Western Reserve University
is a mid-sized research

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.100
institution with lots of
undergrad and grad students

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.560
that all intertwine.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.020
And like all
universities, we have

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:51.430
a vast amount of information
for our students to consume.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:53.830
And our job, as
technologists, is

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:56.810
to bring that information
closer to the students.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:01.560
This saves them time so they
can focus on their learning.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.360
One of the ways that
we're doing that

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.150
is through our chat
bot, Spartan Answers.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.840
Spartan Answers is built on the
technology of Google Assistant,

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.130
using Dialogflow, infused
with our information.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:18.790
So the project to
create Spartan Answers

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.240
was a very unique
project for us.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.250
It wasn't a top-down
approach, like most projects.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.750
Right before the
second quarter of 2018,

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.280
our CIO asked, those of us
in university technology

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:36.610
that we had interest in
chat bot technologies,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:37.870
to just self-group.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:41.200
So we did, and we had a really
good group of people that

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.670
decided, let's talk about this.

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:44.590
Let's figure out.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.510
And we had our own roadmap.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:51.220
We figured out our own
audience and direction to move.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:54.730
And we're really excited
about what happened.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:06:00.130
So that all started around the
first part of second quarter

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:01.660
2018.

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:04.390
And within about a month,
one of our developers

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:07.810
found some code on GitHub,
wrote a web application

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:15.220
using AWS and Amazon Lex, which
worked well as a prototype.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.390
But it didn't allow us to
integrate as quickly as we

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.460
wanted to with our services.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:27.350
And it also didn't work
really well on mobile devices.

00:06:27.350 --> 00:06:29.560
So we knew we had
some work to do.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:33.850
So fast forward to
July, last Cloud Next.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:35.140
I met Vanessa.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.930
And we started
talking about what

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:42.460
Case Western could benefit with
Google and Google technologies.

00:06:42.460 --> 00:06:46.690
And then, in August, Google
released Google Assistant

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.500
to G Suite for Education,
which opened the doors for us

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.860
to use Google Assistant
for our chat bot.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.910
So I started exploring
how to use that.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:02.980
And within a couple of
days, I had feature parity

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.500
with our original prototype.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:07.940
And then, a couple
more days after that,

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:10.660
I was able to integrate with
our location information

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.990
and our campus events
information really easily.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:18.850
This also allowed us to have
easy access on mobile devices

00:07:18.850 --> 00:07:22.660
because Google Assistant already
has an Android and an iOS app.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:24.350
So we were very
excited about that.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.310
And we decided to
switch our platforms

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:29.890
to Google Assistant
and Dialogflow because

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:31.870
of that prototype.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:35.050
So in quarter four
of 2018, we started

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:39.760
demoing that project to
the different faculty

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.440
and staff and our students.

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:45.067
And the student focus groups
gave us a really great idea

00:07:45.067 --> 00:07:46.400
that we were on the right track.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.040
But they also helped us build
our roadmap for the future.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.540
And then, in February, we
released our product to pilot.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.730
And it's been very
well-received.

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.150
And we're very excited about it.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.270
So I want to talk
briefly about where

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:05.870
we're at, how we built it,
and where we're going next.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.230
So to explain where
we're at, I want

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:13.040
you to imagine that you
are a first-time student.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.460
You're stepping foot on
Case Western's campus

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.290
for the first time.

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:18.550
It's August.

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:22.340
The weather's really nice in
Cleveland, Ohio at that time.

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:26.800
So what is the first question
you as a first-time student

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:28.240
might ask?

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:31.510
Well, I know most
first-time students would

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:35.950
ask, how do I connect to Wi-Fi?

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:39.280
So you pull up Sparta
Answers, and you ask it,

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:40.990
how do I connect to Wi-Fi?

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:43.510
And if you see,
on the screen, it

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:45.610
brings up a nice chat interface.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:47.980
In a chat bubble, it
tells you that it found

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:49.390
the following information.

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.830
And then it uses the
material design card view,

00:08:53.830 --> 00:08:56.770
which also allows for a link
that you can click through

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.270
for more information.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.540
So you know how to get on Wi-Fi.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.680
So you turn on Wi-Fi.

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:05.800
And you're, like, OK,
I need to buy my books.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:08.540
So you ask, where's
the bookstore?

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:14.440
So it shows location, a link
to Google Maps for navigation.

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.850
It shows the building
title and picture

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.630
so you know exactly
where you're going.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:23.080
And third, you might want
to know what's happening

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.190
this weekend, so you ask that.

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:27.580
And sure enough, it shows a
list of the events happening

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.670
this weekend so you can
enjoy time with your friends

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:32.960
before classes start.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.890
So with that, I want to
move into how we built it.

00:09:38.890 --> 00:09:41.410
And I know this
might be a little

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:42.970
technical for some people.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.030
But don't worry.

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:46.870
There are a lot
of pieces that you

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.830
don't need to be a
developer to understand.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.070
And I'll walk through those.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:53.800
As Vanessa mentioned,
Dialogflow uses

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.330
a term called intents, which is
just a question and answer set,

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:01.330
so Dialogflow, as
machine learning,

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.870
can understand what to
respond to a certain question.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:08.090
But to get it to respond
with the machine learning,

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:09.460
you have to train it.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:11.020
And the way you
train it is by giving

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:14.200
various types of questions--

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:17.320
various types of ways
to ask the question--

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:20.200
so it can run its machine
learning training on that.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:23.170
And so you see on
the screen, it says,

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.460
how do I connect to Wi-Fi?

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:25.720
Is there Wi-Fi on campus?

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:30.040
And the third one,
does CWRU have Wi-Fi?

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:33.160
And I wanted to point out
the highlight in yellow,

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:38.530
that CWRU is one
of the many ways

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:41.500
that you can say Case
Western Reserve University.

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:45.490
And so it is tagged as
a university intent--

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:49.100
or university entity,
which allows for synonyms.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:50.710
So this is how we
got around that, so

00:10:50.710 --> 00:10:52.990
we didn't have to train
for each type of way

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:57.110
you could ask, how do you get
to Case Western or whatever.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:11:01.360
So once you have it trained, you
have to tell it how to respond.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.180
And if you remember, back
to the Wi-Fi question,

00:11:04.180 --> 00:11:07.030
we had a little chat
bubble that said,

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.570
I found the following
information.

00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.740
So in the simple
response on the left,

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:14.020
that's where you input
that information.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:16.120
And then, in the simple
response on the right,

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:20.980
this is the audio response
that Google Assistant gives.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.530
And then, in the
Basic Card, this

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:27.760
is where it shows the material
design card layout with a link.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:32.740
And that's all it takes to do
one question and answer intent.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.540
It's super easy
and fast, which is

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:37.120
why we really like Dialogflow.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:38.870
Now, I have one more
example for you.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:40.690
And that's the
location question,

00:11:40.690 --> 00:11:42.770
where is the bookstore?

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:46.450
So you'll see that it's
the same type of training.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:50.320
And there's a lot of words
highlighted in orange here.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:53.590
Now, instead of using the
synonym approach with entities,

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:57.770
we're using multiple
locations as an entity,

00:11:57.770 --> 00:12:02.050
so we can train one
question, one intent,

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.510
with all of our locations.

00:12:04.510 --> 00:12:09.040
And we didn't have to input
every building into Dialogflow.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:10.930
So it makes it really easy.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:14.230
Once we have a new building
come online-- which we will,

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:18.070
here in the next few months,
with our health education

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.870
campus.

00:12:19.870 --> 00:12:22.810
once it's inputted in
the campus maps database,

00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:25.330
it will be live right in the
chat bot, which, we're really

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:28.380
excited about that integration.

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:32.230
Now, to do the response
for a question like this,

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:33.550
it's much different.

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:36.130
It's a programmatic way.

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:37.740
You start with
Dialogflow, which is

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.060
what we've been talking about.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:41.850
But the blue box in the
middle is our webhook.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:44.400
And a webhook is
simply a program

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.580
that you write that sits
in between Dialogflow

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:49.190
and other services.

00:12:49.190 --> 00:12:52.050
And in the red box,
the service API

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:54.780
for the location information
question-- that's

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.450
the campus maps database API.

00:12:57.450 --> 00:12:59.970
But we also use it
for Google Calendar.

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.950
It can be any type of service.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:07.090
So once Dialogflow
gets the question,

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:12.450
it sends that question to the
webhook with the intent name

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:13.630
and with the parameters.

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:16.410
So in the case of,
where's the bookstore,

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:20.730
it sends the map intent with
the bookstore as a location.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:27.030
So the webhook knows to talk
to the Google Maps database.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:28.650
And the Google Maps
database looks up

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:31.920
information about the
bookstore and passes it

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:35.340
on back to the webhook, which
then packages it up in a way

00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:38.980
that Dialogflow can understand.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:44.230
So that is how easy it is
to do both of the types

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:46.630
of questions-- one
that is just simple--

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:47.960
you give it the answer--

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:52.460
and the other that it looks
up in different applications.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:55.420
So with that, where
are we going next?

00:13:55.420 --> 00:13:58.280
We know we have a
really good start.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.713
But we know there's so
much more room to grow.

00:14:01.713 --> 00:14:03.880
We're definitely going to
be talking to our students

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.230
for more ideas.

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:09.190
But the few that we're starting
with here in the next month

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:11.770
is our question and
answer input tool.

00:14:11.770 --> 00:14:14.770
So even though it's
easy to input questions

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:18.070
into Dialogflow,
it's not as quick

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:19.810
if you have hundreds
of questions

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:22.460
to input at the same time.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.360
So we're writing,
using Dialogflow APIs,

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:30.130
we're writing a tool that
will allow our departments

00:14:30.130 --> 00:14:32.050
to input hundreds of questions.

00:14:32.050 --> 00:14:34.360
And then our university
marketing and communications

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.780
will verify and approve each
question and each answer.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.800
So it can all be streamlined.

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:44.230
And we can have thousands
of questions in our chat bot

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.570
up and running very quickly
here in the next few months.

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:52.270
So the next two editions
are all using the webhook.

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:56.920
Course information-- just simply
any type of quick information

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:57.550
about a course.

00:14:57.550 --> 00:14:58.660
So where is it?

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:00.530
What day and time?

00:15:00.530 --> 00:15:02.082
Who's teaching?

00:15:02.082 --> 00:15:03.790
This is something our
students asked for,

00:15:03.790 --> 00:15:05.420
so we're excited
to put it in there.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.870
And finally, the shuttle routes.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:11.620
We don't want our students
sitting at a shuttle stop

00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:14.110
waiting and waiting
for a shuttle.

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:18.610
So we're going to be putting
in, where is my shuttle?

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.310
When will the shuttle
be at my stop, which

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:24.650
will save them a lot of time.

00:15:24.650 --> 00:15:29.350
So with all of these examples,
and with all these additions,

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:33.730
we are totally
bringing information

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:36.190
closer to the students.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:39.040
That way, they can
focus on things

00:15:39.040 --> 00:15:41.890
that are more important than
just finding information.

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:43.540
They can focus on
their learning.

00:15:43.540 --> 00:15:44.710
With that, thank you.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:47.032
And I'd like to turn it
over to Daniel McCarthy.

00:15:47.032 --> 00:15:48.490
He's going to tell
you what they're

00:15:48.490 --> 00:15:50.350
doing at Strayer University.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:51.062
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:51.062 --> 00:15:52.270
DANIEL MCCARTHY: Thanks, man.

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:54.670
[APPLAUSE]

00:15:57.637 --> 00:15:59.100
Good afternoon.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:02.210
The company I represent,
Strategic Education, we

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:03.950
actually have two universities.

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:07.700
We have Strayer University
and Capella University.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:12.260
And our student population
are primarily working adults.

00:16:12.260 --> 00:16:14.960
They have jobs during the day.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.390
They come and do their
coursework online

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:22.820
in our learning management
system after hours.

00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:26.060
So I'm here to talk to you
today about conversational

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:29.782
self-service and what we've
done at Strayer University.

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:35.470
At Strayer, one of the problems
we were trying to solve--

00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:37.390
or the problem we
were trying to solve--

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:40.870
was volume, variance,
and availability.

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:44.140
So we have working adults.

00:16:44.140 --> 00:16:48.400
They aren't typically able to
make it into the university

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.830
or call the university
during working hours.

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:56.450
When they do make it in, or
when they are able to call in,

00:16:56.450 --> 00:16:57.950
they may get bounced
from department

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:01.910
to department and, again--

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:03.830
hold times, wait times.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:06.619
And then finally, we're
getting more service requests

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:09.480
than we have staff to support.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.869
So a chat bot's kind of
a near-perfect solution

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:14.790
to a problem like that.

00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:18.300
A chat bot can be
available at all times

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.170
of the day, 24 hours a day.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:24.250
It can handle an
infinite amount of scale.

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:29.500
And trained properly, it
can cross domain boundaries.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:32.470
If you think about this outside
of the education industry,

00:17:32.470 --> 00:17:33.620
for example--

00:17:33.620 --> 00:17:35.270
just think about
HR for a second.

00:17:35.270 --> 00:17:40.570
Within HR, you might have
benefits management and then

00:17:40.570 --> 00:17:41.710
payroll.

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:43.930
Those are two different
domains with staff

00:17:43.930 --> 00:17:47.740
that are trained specifically
for those domains.

00:17:47.740 --> 00:17:50.530
And you might have to be
bounced from department

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:51.626
to the department.

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:59.670
Our timeline for the
initial release of our chat

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:03.390
bot, Irving, which was named
after the founder of Strayer

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.990
University, Irving Strayer,
was very compressed.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:11.910
You'll see here, roughly six
months from idea to pilot

00:18:11.910 --> 00:18:13.815
to production and then scaling.

00:18:17.290 --> 00:18:20.660
What enabled this was a
little bit of a perfect storm.

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:23.260
So those developing
this solution,

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:28.540
myself included, had the domain
experience for the domain

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:32.110
that we were going to hit
first, the technical experience,

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:35.230
and then combining that
with Dialogflow and GCP

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:41.010
allowed us to move very quickly
from an idea to a prototype

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:42.145
to production.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:48.820
Now, we'll give you a
little bit of a demo.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:49.660
Now, I apologize.

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:53.410
I'm going to start and
stop this a few times

00:18:53.410 --> 00:18:57.970
so that I can talk through
it just a little bit.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:03.810
So I've already stopped it.

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:05.560
Isn't that disappointing?

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:06.060
All right.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:09.480
So Irving is available
in our student portal

00:19:09.480 --> 00:19:12.464
where almost all of
our learners come.

00:19:12.464 --> 00:19:13.940
AUDIENCE: It's
[? right ?] there.

00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:16.170
DANIEL MCCARTHY: Oh, thank you.

00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:17.010
All right.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:21.146
So still going.

00:19:21.146 --> 00:19:22.860
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:25.080
DANIEL MCCARTHY: All right.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:28.870
We're going to go back
here and do this again.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:30.120
All right.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:33.570
I'll talk a little
bit before we play it.

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.990
Irving is available in the
student portal online, the web

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:38.700
application.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:40.960
We're piloting Google
Assistant right now.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.290
And we're also piloting SMS.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:47.190
So meeting the students when
they are and where they are.

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:48.060
When they are--

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.410
24 hours a day.

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:53.520
Where they are-- whatever
platform that they

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:56.130
want to interact with.

00:19:56.130 --> 00:19:58.270
Some of those a little
bit easier than others.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:03.840
And now, with that,
we will play the demo.

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.460
As students interact
with Irving,

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:08.070
it recognizes that, if
they've used Irving before.

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:10.710
So you'll see here Holly,
who is a learner that

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:14.730
is interested in her academic
progress and completion

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:18.510
or progress towards graduation,
has used Irving before.

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:20.700
She wants to know what
classes she has remaining.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:23.580
Irving goes into our
back-office SIS system

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:26.310
and pulls up her
program, how she's

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:29.330
progressed through that program,
and what she has outstanding.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.280
Now, you'll see
here, as Holly asks

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:35.340
about the syllabus for a
course that she's already

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:38.230
registered for, that
she's using the title.

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:40.230
And this ties into a
little bit of what Greg was

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:42.310
talking about with entities.

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:44.820
So we can take the
title of a course,

00:20:44.820 --> 00:20:48.720
we can take the course ID, and
we can use those as synonyms.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:52.260
And you'll see, in the response,
that Dialogflow and Irving have

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:54.120
recognized that
critical thinking is

00:20:54.120 --> 00:21:00.650
synonymous with PHI 210.

00:21:00.650 --> 00:21:05.020
And in this case, Irving is
giving the student the link out

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:06.520
to the course syllabus.

00:21:06.520 --> 00:21:09.370
Because would you ever
deliver a course syllabus

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:12.050
in a conversation
in its entirety?

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:12.550
No.

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:14.740
You want to keep things
brief and conversational

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:16.240
as you go through that.

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:17.848
Now, Holly's concerned
with her GPA.

00:21:17.848 --> 00:21:20.140
So again, we're going to go
into our back-office system

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:23.172
and pull up that GPA
and see what she's got.

00:21:23.172 --> 00:21:25.630
This is a little verbose, and
may need a little bit of work

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:27.070
regarding the response here.

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:30.520
But Holly's doing all
right with a 3.6 GPA.

00:21:30.520 --> 00:21:32.560
And she's in
satisfactory standing

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:35.401
both academically
and financially.

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:40.630
Now, Holly's asking
about her grades.

00:21:40.630 --> 00:21:42.610
You'll notice that she
didn't give a term.

00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:43.825
Schools are term-based right?

00:21:43.825 --> 00:21:46.480
It doesn't matter-- semesters,
trimesters, quarters, what

00:21:46.480 --> 00:21:48.190
have you-- they're term-based.

00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:50.170
Irving has contextual awareness.

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:53.530
We have set it up such
that Irving assumes

00:21:53.530 --> 00:21:55.480
the most relevant
term based on the time

00:21:55.480 --> 00:21:57.100
that the question is asked.

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:58.840
We're going to go
through it again here.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:00.340
And again, we have entities.

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:02.950
Holly asks about
grades for fall.

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:04.930
But again, that's a
little bit nebulous.

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:07.420
Is it fall '18 or fall '19?

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:08.890
Which one makes most sense?

00:22:08.890 --> 00:22:10.390
Again, contextual
awareness allows

00:22:10.390 --> 00:22:13.600
us to know that we're
talking about fall '18.

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:16.690
And great job, Holly,
for getting A's

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:19.120
in both your courses.

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:23.040
Now, when am I
going to graduate?

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:24.390
It's a hard projection, right?

00:22:24.390 --> 00:22:27.090
It's really based
on student input.

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:29.580
What are you going to
put into it as a student?

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:33.300
So Irving goes through and
calculates the average courses

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:36.210
completed per term, and then
asks Holly how many she's

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:38.380
going to continue completing.

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:40.350
And then, we're a
quarter-based school,

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:42.480
so how many quarters
throughout the year

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:44.100
are you planning to attend?

00:22:44.100 --> 00:22:47.010
And from that, Irving
will then calculate

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:54.940
an anticipated
graduation date and give

00:22:54.940 --> 00:22:57.700
the projection and a
little bit of legalese

00:22:57.700 --> 00:23:00.920
for those that require it.

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:07.030
So now, we'll talk a
little bit about surveying.

00:23:07.030 --> 00:23:09.413
And you'll see here that
Holly is saying thanks.

00:23:09.413 --> 00:23:11.080
And that's a little
bit of an indication

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:13.570
that the conversation is over,
that the student, the user,

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:16.480
has gotten what they want
from the conversation.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:17.830
And that's rare.

00:23:17.830 --> 00:23:22.400
How many times have you
guys been in a conversation

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:26.180
within any chat and just left
it when you got what you wanted?

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:27.500
I have.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:29.960
And that that's the
more frequent case.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:32.030
You just leave the conversation.

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:32.750
Like, I'm done.

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:33.650
I got what I needed--

00:23:33.650 --> 00:23:36.110
no, thank you, or
anything like that.

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:43.570
So we survey at the time
of conversation conclusion.

00:23:43.570 --> 00:23:46.150
So when we recognize
with a goodbye,

00:23:46.150 --> 00:23:49.820
with a thank you, or
something along those lines,

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:51.100
which is a rare occasion.

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:52.810
The other time that
we really survey

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:54.940
is when we know
that we have failed

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:56.800
to understand the student.

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:59.560
And that happens,
within Strayer,

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:01.810
when we have failed
sequentially three times.

00:24:01.810 --> 00:24:04.150
Because we really try
to understand as best

00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:06.380
we can-- give them
as many opportunities

00:24:06.380 --> 00:24:09.340
to let us help them
with automation.

00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:11.920
But when we fail
them three times,

00:24:11.920 --> 00:24:13.300
we'll give them a survey.

00:24:13.300 --> 00:24:15.880
And we will transition
if we have availability

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:18.070
to a human agent so
that the human agent can

00:24:18.070 --> 00:24:19.540
continue that conversation.

00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:22.120
And the human agent will
receive the full transcript.

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:24.580
But we're talking about surveys.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:27.610
So we survey more
often when we know

00:24:27.610 --> 00:24:31.390
we've failed than when we
know we've been successful.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:39.365
And as we get into
that, we'll talk

00:24:39.365 --> 00:24:41.240
a little bit about those
numbers and how that

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:43.590
plays into the survey results.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:46.970
So we've had over 400,000
conversations with Irving.

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:54.560
Over 80% of all chat requests
are handled within Irving

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:57.005
without transitioning
to a live agent.

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:03.840
This is where the
surveys come in.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.780
Of those that have been
surveyed by Irving,

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:09.540
87% agree that
Irving helped them

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:12.297
answer their questions easily.

00:25:12.297 --> 00:25:14.130
Now, we've gotten some
really good feedback,

00:25:14.130 --> 00:25:16.810
and we've gotten some negative
feedback in there as well--

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:20.880
all constructive-- and will
help us considerably to improve

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:22.110
the Irving experience.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:26.680
So Greg talked a little
bit little bit about it.

00:25:26.680 --> 00:25:29.060
Vanessa talked a
little bit about it--

00:25:29.060 --> 00:25:29.830
intents.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:32.740
Intents are-- you can think of
those kind of as topical areas.

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.110
We have 1,400 intents
in production right now.

00:25:39.950 --> 00:25:45.280
Now, this chart is
a bit of an eyesore.

00:25:45.280 --> 00:25:47.500
But I want you to think
in terms of intents.

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:50.840
This chart shows you,
over the past 30 days,

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.980
how our intents have been used
by our student population.

00:25:53.980 --> 00:25:56.560
And look at that-- you
got 20 or 30 intents that

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:58.870
are receiving the most volume.

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:02.020
But you look on the
right, the long tail--

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:02.950
look at that.

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:06.130
All other intents
represent the majority

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.650
of the questions
that are answered.

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:10.780
What you should
take away from that

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:15.100
is that the superb
service with a chat bot--

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:17.350
it's coming on the right.

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:19.120
For all of those
questions, they didn't

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:21.220
have to go somewhere
else to get an answer.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:24.760
They were able to get it
within, even though the 20 to 30

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:29.483
there are the most
frequently used.

00:26:29.483 --> 00:26:30.900
I'm going to stop
going backwards.

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:32.370
I promise.

00:26:32.370 --> 00:26:34.570
So how do we do this?

00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:39.930
So this is going to be very
similar to a typical software

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.880
development lifecycle.

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:43.680
You got to understand
your use case.

00:26:43.680 --> 00:26:46.900
When you define your use
case, be very granular.

00:26:46.900 --> 00:26:48.840
You want you want to
be as specific, when

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:51.480
you start any
conversational experience,

00:26:51.480 --> 00:26:53.290
as you possibly can.

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:56.173
And then you need to gather
data on that conversation.

00:26:56.173 --> 00:26:57.840
How do your students,
how do your users,

00:26:57.840 --> 00:26:59.970
ask about this topic?

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.680
What parameters are
they providing to it?

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:03.750
What context are they giving?

00:27:03.750 --> 00:27:05.785
And then you need to
script your conversations.

00:27:05.785 --> 00:27:07.410
And when you script
your conversations,

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:10.045
you start with the happy path--

00:27:10.045 --> 00:27:11.670
the happy path being,
how you want them

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.150
to go through the conversation?

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:15.600
But then you need to think
about gracefully handling

00:27:15.600 --> 00:27:20.180
the fallout, the fall backs, the
branches of the conversation,

00:27:20.180 --> 00:27:23.835
and allowing for
that contextually.

00:27:23.835 --> 00:27:26.210
Then, you're going to start
developing your integrations,

00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:29.450
you're SIS systems,
your financial systems,

00:27:29.450 --> 00:27:32.300
your human resource
systems, any system.

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:37.890
And you're going to test those
integrations with Dialogflow.

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:41.070
Five-- that's your
most important step.

00:27:41.070 --> 00:27:44.430
You must pilot with
your user population.

00:27:44.430 --> 00:27:46.890
If you do not part pilot
with your user population,

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:49.350
you will get some very
unpleasant surprises

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.680
when you take this into any
kind of production environment.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:55.890
We do this regularly
at Strayer University.

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:58.440
And every time, we
learn something new--

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:02.070
a new way that they ask about it
that we haven't trained the bot

00:28:02.070 --> 00:28:07.890
on or a new use case that we
can then take back to number one

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:10.390
and begin developing again.

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:10.890
All right.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:12.780
So we've gone one through five.

00:28:12.780 --> 00:28:14.148
And now we're in production.

00:28:14.148 --> 00:28:14.940
We can all go home.

00:28:14.940 --> 00:28:15.870
We're done.

00:28:15.870 --> 00:28:17.980
It's not quite that simple.

00:28:17.980 --> 00:28:21.840
So when you're in production,
you need to monitor your bot.

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:24.450
You need to look for errors
within your integrations,

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:27.690
and more importantly, fallbacks
within your conversations.

00:28:27.690 --> 00:28:30.600
And fallbacks are when the
bot can't understand what

00:28:30.600 --> 00:28:32.310
the users are sending to it.

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:34.440
Because within those
fallbacks, that's

00:28:34.440 --> 00:28:36.300
where you're going to
find that long tail.

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:41.320
What do they want to talk
to your school about?

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:43.573
That is where you find this out.

00:28:43.573 --> 00:28:45.240
And they're going to
keep repeating this

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:49.290
until you maybe run out
of conversations to have.

00:28:49.290 --> 00:28:52.350
Our experience is that you're
likely not going to run out

00:28:52.350 --> 00:28:53.520
of conversations to have.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:59.110
How do we do this within GCP?

00:28:59.110 --> 00:29:01.120
Well, Dialogflow is
the system that we

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:06.070
use for the intent detection
and entity extraction.

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:09.100
Then we use Cloud Functions for
fulfillment and conversation

00:29:09.100 --> 00:29:10.480
routing.

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:13.720
We use Cloud SQL to
record every interaction

00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:18.590
that we have within Irving.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:20.900
On a nightly basis, we
put that into BigQuery

00:29:20.900 --> 00:29:23.040
so that we can do some
additional analysis.

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:26.570
And we use Data Studio
for the reporting there.

00:29:26.570 --> 00:29:27.903
We use Compute Engine currently.

00:29:27.903 --> 00:29:29.612
Although, there was
an announcement today

00:29:29.612 --> 00:29:30.500
that may change that.

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:33.710
We use Compute
Engine to post Redis

00:29:33.710 --> 00:29:35.780
so that we can do caching.

00:29:35.780 --> 00:29:39.530
And then we have a
custom AutoML model

00:29:39.530 --> 00:29:43.040
that allows us to do fallback
classification to better

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:44.540
the experience.

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:47.770
And since I am
already out of time,

00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:50.420
anyone who wants to hear about
that, we can follow up after.

00:29:53.930 --> 00:29:56.930
All of those components
are put together here.

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:59.120
And even this is a
little bit high-level

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:01.160
on how we combined all
of those components

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:03.690
into an architecture
that allows us to serve

00:30:03.690 --> 00:30:05.990
1,400 different intents.

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:08.240
And again, I'll be
available for a little bit

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:11.730
afterwards if you have
questions on this.

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:14.230
So where are we
going to go next?

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:16.170
Well, this week,
we are launching,

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:18.210
at our sister
university, Capella,

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:22.650
we're launching Ella in a
pilot phase-- very excited

00:30:22.650 --> 00:30:23.920
about that.

00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:26.700
We're also going to be
increasing the personalization

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:29.220
both within the integrations
that we're developing

00:30:29.220 --> 00:30:32.220
and the ability to interject
within conversations

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:34.500
and maybe not even wait for
a conversation to start.

00:30:37.650 --> 00:30:40.500
Next, we're going to go into
our learning management system,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:42.960
where our students spend
the majority of their time,

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:46.050
to assist them in the
classroom experience--

00:30:46.050 --> 00:30:50.760
completing assignments, writing
assistance, maybe even quizzes,

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:54.970
and then, finally, phone
system integration.

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:57.210
So the, press one for
the first circle of hell,

00:30:57.210 --> 00:30:59.400
press two for the
second circle of hell--

00:30:59.400 --> 00:31:04.590
we're going to replace that
with an integration with Irving

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:06.660
so that you can use
natural language

00:31:06.660 --> 00:31:11.620
to better the experience.

00:31:11.620 --> 00:31:14.180
And with that, I'll hand
it over to Neil Gomes.

00:31:14.180 --> 00:31:16.140
And he can talk to you
about their experience

00:31:16.140 --> 00:31:17.934
at Thomas Jefferson.

00:31:17.934 --> 00:31:20.796
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:24.292 --> 00:31:25.750
NEIL GOMES: I'm
going to be talking

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:30.040
to you about the value of chat
bots in the medical space, both

00:31:30.040 --> 00:31:35.110
for medical and education,
as well as, actually, maybe

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:37.900
at some point in the
future, seeing a chat

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:39.906
bot as your doctor.

00:31:39.906 --> 00:31:42.380
I'm sure everybody
will like that.

00:31:42.380 --> 00:31:42.880
No?

00:31:42.880 --> 00:31:43.380
No?

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:45.120
I see some faces saying no.

00:31:45.120 --> 00:31:46.330
OK.

00:31:46.330 --> 00:31:48.050
Just you wait.

00:31:48.050 --> 00:31:50.410
[LAUGHS]

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:55.600
Maybe at some Google Next,
we'll be laughing at this

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:59.300
and saying, hey, I said that
you'd see a chat bot as a doc.

00:31:59.300 --> 00:31:59.800
OK.

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:00.310
All right.

00:32:00.310 --> 00:32:03.430
Well, so what I wanted
to impress upon you today

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:08.190
is really the power
of conversation.

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:10.920
So I'm going to ask
you a quick question.

00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:13.900
OK, and the answer is not iOS.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:15.670
It's not Android.

00:32:15.670 --> 00:32:18.250
It's not Windows.

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:20.590
It's not Chrome.

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:23.920
What is the best operating
system that we currently

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:26.620
understand really well?

00:32:26.620 --> 00:32:27.307
What's that?

00:32:27.307 --> 00:32:28.140
AUDIENCE: The brain?

00:32:28.140 --> 00:32:29.280
NEIL GOMES: The brain.

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:30.980
Not really.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:34.970
I can't tell what's happening
in your brain right now.

00:32:34.970 --> 00:32:36.130
But what else?

00:32:36.130 --> 00:32:36.683
Yeah.

00:32:36.683 --> 00:32:38.225
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:38.225 --> 00:32:39.100
NEIL GOMES: Language.

00:32:39.100 --> 00:32:39.890
Conversation.

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:41.646
The cue is right there.

00:32:41.646 --> 00:32:43.550
OK.

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:46.390
There isn't much of a
learning curve, right?

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:51.100
And you don't need to teach
somebody how to speak.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.650
Usually, after the age of--

00:32:53.650 --> 00:32:58.480
in my kid my kids' case, 0.5--

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.720
they know how to converse.

00:33:00.720 --> 00:33:06.430
And so I think we've been
sitting on this for a long time

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:08.590
and haven't really
started leveraging it.

00:33:08.590 --> 00:33:10.540
It's really, really powerful.

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:12.880
And being able to
get machines to start

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:17.410
engaging in conversation is
actually a huge leap for us

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:23.130
in terms of human development.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:24.650
So I think that's
really important.

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:26.670
We need to understand that.

00:33:26.670 --> 00:33:29.980
And I founded this group called
the DICE Group, or the Digital

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:31.750
Innovation and Consumer
Experience Group

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:34.780
at Thomas Jefferson University
and Jefferson Health "where

00:33:34.780 --> 00:33:36.340
we bring digital to life."

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:39.310
That's our motto.

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:41.560
And so we've been
we've been doing

00:33:41.560 --> 00:33:43.718
a lot of work in
the chat bot space

00:33:43.718 --> 00:33:45.260
that I'll talk to
you about in a bit.

00:33:45.260 --> 00:33:48.190
But to give you some
context, this is who we are.

00:33:48.190 --> 00:33:50.500
Thomas Jefferson University
is the third or fourth

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:52.060
medical school in the nation--

00:33:52.060 --> 00:33:54.157
almost a 200-year-old
institution.

00:33:54.157 --> 00:33:55.990
You've heard of terms
like "gross anatomy"--

00:33:55.990 --> 00:33:58.210
Samuel Gross was the
Jefferson physician

00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:00.280
who started gross anatomy.

00:34:00.280 --> 00:34:02.530
The heart-lung machine
was invented at Jefferson.

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:04.900
And we have about
15 hospitals now.

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:08.530
We've been in a rapid
growth kind of phase, mode--

00:34:08.530 --> 00:34:12.880
and on a revenue of about
$5.5 billion at this point.

00:34:12.880 --> 00:34:15.037
But the most important
figures to us

00:34:15.037 --> 00:34:16.120
are really those patients.

00:34:16.120 --> 00:34:19.960
We see about 3.2
million patients a year

00:34:19.960 --> 00:34:23.800
in our outpatient clinics,
about 150,000 in our hospitals,

00:34:23.800 --> 00:34:28.210
and about 550,000 in our ERs.

00:34:28.210 --> 00:34:30.190
And we have about
8,000 students--

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:32.889
so a lot of impact through
some of the solutions

00:34:32.889 --> 00:34:34.929
that we create as a group.

00:34:34.929 --> 00:34:37.090
Our team is about 220 people.

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:42.949
We've grown from about 2 when we
started 4 and a half years ago.

00:34:42.949 --> 00:34:45.358
These are people that
are software developers.

00:34:45.358 --> 00:34:46.900
Rob, who's sitting
in the front here,

00:34:46.900 --> 00:34:49.080
runs our software
development team.

00:34:49.080 --> 00:34:51.590
And we have about 70 or
so software developers.

00:34:51.590 --> 00:34:53.530
We have designers,
human-centered designers,

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:57.550
about 40 of them, that take
up solutions that come to us,

00:34:57.550 --> 00:34:59.505
and then find what
the real problem is.

00:34:59.505 --> 00:35:01.130
And only then do we
develop a solution.

00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:01.957
OK?

00:35:01.957 --> 00:35:04.540
And we develop several different
types of solutions like this,

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:07.660
about 130 a year,
and return about 10x,

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:11.530
which is something I learned
about at Google last year,

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:13.660
in terms of returns on
most of our projects

00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:17.110
because we do things
very frugally--

00:35:17.110 --> 00:35:18.610
very frugal innovation.

00:35:18.610 --> 00:35:20.925
And we return a lot of
value to the organization.

00:35:20.925 --> 00:35:22.300
Just on our digital
solutions, we

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:25.570
save the organization over
$40 million or so per year

00:35:25.570 --> 00:35:27.470
and have done a bunch
of other things.

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:31.120
And sometimes you'd be surprised
at what generates value.

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:32.260
It's amazing.

00:35:32.260 --> 00:35:37.600
Our donation app generated about
$1.46 million just last month.

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:40.540
So we never thought
that would happen.

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:43.390
What we do with digital
is, we improve access,

00:35:43.390 --> 00:35:46.090
make it convenient to get to
us, or for us to get to somebody

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:48.490
else where they are--
that's always better--

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:50.780
and then provide closed-loop
digital experiences,

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:53.240
which is very rare in
the health care space.

00:35:53.240 --> 00:35:54.700
So we try to do that.

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:57.880
And chat bots and
such types of tools--

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:00.730
voice chat bots also
enable you to do that.

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:02.550
And I'll give you
a quick example.

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:04.720
So voice bots, right?

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:06.593
Powered by a Google Dialogflow.

00:36:06.593 --> 00:36:08.260
We'll be talking about
this on Thursday.

00:36:08.260 --> 00:36:11.970
Rob's going to do
the presentation.

00:36:11.970 --> 00:36:14.680
We felt that there
was tremendous value

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:16.990
to our patients if we
were able to answer

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:19.070
any of their
questions at any time

00:36:19.070 --> 00:36:21.040
while they are in
the hospital room,

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:23.440
even though there was no nurse
available or no physician

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:24.410
available.

00:36:24.410 --> 00:36:28.150
So we made a hospital
room chat bot.

00:36:28.150 --> 00:36:31.030
And it's voice--
we got a speaker

00:36:31.030 --> 00:36:32.170
system that really worked.

00:36:32.170 --> 00:36:36.160
We experimented with a lot of
different types of speakers.

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:40.990
People stole a lot of
our speakers [LAUGHS]

00:36:40.990 --> 00:36:43.780
speaker cables disappeared.

00:36:43.780 --> 00:36:47.170
And then we came down to
this particular form factor

00:36:47.170 --> 00:36:48.850
with the company
that we work with.

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:52.630
And it tells you
a lot of things.

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.190
It tells you when
your meal might

00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:56.980
be coming, who your
doctor is, whether they're

00:36:56.980 --> 00:36:57.910
board certified not.

00:37:00.880 --> 00:37:05.480
It will tell you about
your disease or condition.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:07.840
It'll give you your
discharge instructions,

00:37:07.840 --> 00:37:10.810
if you're leaving, and narrate
them out to you from the EMR.

00:37:10.810 --> 00:37:12.650
It will control your TV.

00:37:12.650 --> 00:37:14.590
We have mostly
Samsung Smart TVs.

00:37:14.590 --> 00:37:17.880
And so it controls-- we've
found a channel to do that.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:20.650
Controls the TV, changes
the volume, all that--

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:21.373
lighting.

00:37:21.373 --> 00:37:24.040
We can also have it control HVAC
through our building automation

00:37:24.040 --> 00:37:24.730
systems--

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:27.280
all just through voice.

00:37:27.280 --> 00:37:29.770
So that's our voice bots.

00:37:29.770 --> 00:37:35.410
We found that people that
agree to do clinical research,

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:38.192
they hate the surveys.

00:37:38.192 --> 00:37:39.400
I mean, they are long things.

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:40.940
They lie to you at
the beginning and say

00:37:40.940 --> 00:37:42.357
this will be over
in five minutes,

00:37:42.357 --> 00:37:44.740
but it doesn't get
over in five minutes.

00:37:44.740 --> 00:37:47.170
But they'd rather engage
in conversation, sometimes

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:48.550
even with a chat bot.

00:37:48.550 --> 00:37:51.820
So we made some research chat
bots for our researchers.

00:37:51.820 --> 00:37:54.010
And we have the service
available to them

00:37:54.010 --> 00:37:56.690
to use anytime they want.

00:37:56.690 --> 00:37:59.910
We are now experimenting with
a differential diagnosis chat

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:00.720
bot.

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:03.820
Differential diagnosis
is, whenever your doc--

00:38:03.820 --> 00:38:06.250
you go to the ER, and the doc
says, or the primary care,

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:08.470
and the doc says,
I'll be right back--

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:12.040
they're going to check their
differential diagnosis tool.

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:13.660
Sometimes that's called Google.

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:19.690
But there are actual tools that
they check that give them--

00:38:19.690 --> 00:38:24.010
they put in your
presenting conditions,

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:27.520
what it seems like you might--

00:38:27.520 --> 00:38:29.620
not conditions,
really, but symptoms.

00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:35.140
And then it gives them potential
conditions with probabilities

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:36.320
next to that.

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:37.570
And it's very, very important.

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:39.670
Because sometimes you
might miss something,

00:38:39.670 --> 00:38:41.710
like Lyme disease
for example, which

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:47.200
presents in a very simple
way in an ER many times.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:50.310
And you might just forget to
ask that other question like,

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:53.140
were you in the woods recently?

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:54.490
Did you get a tick bite?

00:38:54.490 --> 00:38:56.230
Those types of
things-- so it prompts

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:59.020
docs to ask these
types of questions.

00:38:59.020 --> 00:39:01.090
And it's an
extremely useful tool

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.765
for learning for
residents and students.

00:39:03.765 --> 00:39:05.140
Because sometimes
they just don't

00:39:05.140 --> 00:39:07.000
realize they've got
to ask that question.

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:09.190
So they go and do this.

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:13.000
What if we could present that
as a chat bot, almost in simple,

00:39:13.000 --> 00:39:14.470
quick, and easy ways?

00:39:14.470 --> 00:39:17.680
Especially because chat
bots have the capability

00:39:17.680 --> 00:39:20.650
to represent canned responses
that they just tap on,

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:22.988
versus having to type
something in, right?

00:39:22.988 --> 00:39:24.780
So that's what we are--
we're experimenting

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:27.070
with a company
called Isabel that

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:29.590
creates a great differential
diagnosis tool that's

00:39:29.590 --> 00:39:33.550
named after the CEO'S
daughter, who passed away

00:39:33.550 --> 00:39:37.930
because a good differential
diagnosis tool was not used.

00:39:37.930 --> 00:39:41.860
So we also created a--

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:44.680
along with one of our students--

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:50.710
once created a compendium
of all medical school notes.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:52.063
OK?

00:39:52.063 --> 00:39:53.980
Came with this brilliant
idea-- got permission

00:39:53.980 --> 00:39:58.510
from the faculty
and the teachers.

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:00.200
And they said it's
fine to do it.

00:40:00.200 --> 00:40:03.070
So they made a Wikipedia of
all medical school notes.

00:40:03.070 --> 00:40:05.980
And so now we're building
chat bots on top of that

00:40:05.980 --> 00:40:09.280
because we have a huge
data source to mine.

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:11.890
We also are-- this
is very inspiring

00:40:11.890 --> 00:40:14.470
to see both examples of
student experience chat bots.

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:18.580
So this is a space we really,
really want to get into.

00:40:18.580 --> 00:40:22.120
We've done some of these things
for patients, but not yet

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:23.420
in the student space.

00:40:23.420 --> 00:40:25.000
So that's something
we want to build.

00:40:25.000 --> 00:40:26.860
We've also just
deployed, recently,

00:40:26.860 --> 00:40:28.423
appointment chat bots.

00:40:28.423 --> 00:40:30.465
The Rothman Institute,
which is part of Jefferson

00:40:30.465 --> 00:40:35.270
and one of the best orthopedic
hospitals in the nation,

00:40:35.270 --> 00:40:37.930
now only takes
online appointments

00:40:37.930 --> 00:40:41.650
through a chat bot, 24/7.

00:40:41.650 --> 00:40:43.420
They do have a phone
number you can call.

00:40:43.420 --> 00:40:45.730
But any online appointments--
there's no form.

00:40:45.730 --> 00:40:48.350
You just do it via chat bot.

00:40:48.350 --> 00:40:52.180
And they're seeing great
engagement and very fast

00:40:52.180 --> 00:40:54.610
information collection.

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:58.740
And finally, a topic that, in
the future we're probably going

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:01.180
to have to deal with-- and
we are doing this right now--

00:41:01.180 --> 00:41:05.100
is a lot of people, especially
institutions like ours,

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:10.540
are investing in Robotic
Process Automation, right, RPA--

00:41:10.540 --> 00:41:14.060
in the financial
side, sometimes even

00:41:14.060 --> 00:41:18.290
in deep clinical areas, things
that happen on the back end.

00:41:18.290 --> 00:41:20.720
And we like to
call these autobots

00:41:20.720 --> 00:41:25.520
because this is pointed at
automation and automation

00:41:25.520 --> 00:41:26.510
platforms.

00:41:26.510 --> 00:41:29.960
And we think there's
great scope here

00:41:29.960 --> 00:41:32.960
because a lot of people that
are in the operational spaces

00:41:32.960 --> 00:41:35.540
don't understand how
these things work.

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:38.300
And the bots could
provide that insight.

00:41:38.300 --> 00:41:41.510
The bots could also
initiate the next action.

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:43.510
If you build the right
type of platform,

00:41:43.510 --> 00:41:48.500
you can do some of these things,
transitionary elements, really,

00:41:48.500 --> 00:41:50.900
really effectively
using bots, we think.

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:54.680
Because when we go into clinical
areas with our design teams,

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:56.510
and we try to make
them more efficient,

00:41:56.510 --> 00:41:59.840
we find that most latency
and most issues with service

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:02.690
happen when something
transitions from one person

00:42:02.690 --> 00:42:03.507
to another.

00:42:03.507 --> 00:42:05.090
So now we're starting
to automate some

00:42:05.090 --> 00:42:06.620
of those transitions, right?

00:42:06.620 --> 00:42:08.360
But some of them
are still human.

00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:11.030
So if you want
somebody to respond,

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:15.110
you have a bot in between
that initiates that next step,

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:18.920
either via text message
or via a platform that you

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:20.710
might have across the system--

00:42:20.710 --> 00:42:22.460
so make things happen
a lot faster, which,

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:23.752
in medicine, is very important.

00:42:23.752 --> 00:42:25.550
Time is life many times.

00:42:25.550 --> 00:42:28.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

